## Context

The observer is a small local web server the user keeps open beside their agent sessions: one per user per machine, showing every project. Four facts shape it. First, it runs alongside the pipeline but is never part of it: the recording side keeps each session's log, and the observer only reads those logs, receives copies of new events and shows them. Second, a server bound to the local machine is still reachable from any web page the user visits, and a domain name can be pointed at the local address to look same-origin, so "localhost only" is no boundary by itself. Third, it can start at any moment (late, after a crash, or because a newer AIDE took over) while events keep arriving from several sessions in several projects; the order in which it learns past and new events decides whether each shows exactly once. Fourth, sessions from different projects, possibly on different AIDE versions, share the one server, and each project's files must stay visible only inside that project's own view.

The address must stay put. The user bookmarks it and every session hands it out, so a server that drifts to another port, or a second one beside the first, leaves the user watching a page that is no longer live.

## Strategy

**One fixed address, never moved.** The port is built in and only an environment override changes it, so every session and the terminal agree on the address with no per-project setting and no port-picking step. A port held by another program ends the start with the port named, never a fallback: a moved server is one the user's bookmark and the sessions' reports no longer reach.

**Check the one per-user record before binding.** A start first reads the per-user record of the running observer and probes it with its token; if it answers, nothing new starts and the caller gets the link to its own session's view. The record is written only after the bind succeeds and removed on shutdown only if it still names this server, so a failed start neither strands a stale address nor erases a live one.

**Newer replaces older; older never replaces newer.** The record carries the running version. A newer caller sends a token-protected shutdown request, waits for that process to exit, then starts on the same port; a same or newer server is kept. It runs one way so two versions cannot swap the server back and forth; an old server that will not exit is named, never killed. Every event is on disk, so the replay loses nothing. The shutdown is a tool handshake, never a page control.

**Open the door before reading history, then merge.** The observer accepts posts and publishes its record before replaying every project's session logs, holding posts until the replay ends, then merges both keyed on session and order. An event recorded mid-replay sits in a log the replay reads, arrives as a post, or both; the merge keeps one copy, in order. Publishing after the replay leaves a gap: an event lands in a log already read and is never posted.

**Every request proves it came from this user's machine.** The observer listens on the loopback address only and refuses any request whose host is not that address and port, defeating a domain pointed at the local address. A post or a shutdown request must also carry the token kept in the user's own app data, and a post must be JSON; a cross-site page can do neither without a preflight the observer never grants.

**Home lists projects; a session view stays inside its project.** The home view lists every project with recorded sessions, live and past. A session's view is built from the root its project's newest session reported, since the shared server has no project of its own; its board comes from that session too, and a terminal brain override applies only to the project it was run from. Every file read resolves inside that root and is refused outside it, however written.

**Reads rebuild; one request changes anything.** Each view is recomputed from its project's files, held events and board on every event and page load, never by watching files, so a missed event shortens history but never stales status. A post is validated against the shared event schema and held in memory; the recording side stays the logs' only writer. The one write deletes a past session's log, refused while it is live. A bad line or post is rejected alone.

## Good examples

- The user has a session open in one project with the observer running, then boots a second project. Its start finds the running observer, starts nothing, and returns the link to the new session's view; the home view now lists both projects, each opening onto its own tree and board.
- A session on a newer AIDE boots while an older observer runs. It asks the old one to shut down, waits for it to exit, and starts on the same port; the replay reads every project's logs, including two events posted mid-replay, and each shows once. When the older session next calls start, it finds the newer server and keeps it.
- In one project's session view, a request asks for a file through a path that climbs into a neighboring project with parent-directory steps. It is refused; the view keeps serving.

## Bad examples

- The fixed port is held by another dev server, so the observer quietly binds the next free port and prints it. The next session's start reports the fixed address, and the user's bookmark shows someone else's app.
- Any version difference triggers a replacement, so an older session booting after a newer one shuts the newer server down. The two sessions swap the server on every start, and each swap drops every open tab's live stream.
- A session view resolves requested files against the folder of the session that started the server instead of its own project's reported root. The second project's view shows the first project's plan as its own.

## References

- User decision on one observer per machine, relayed by the orchestrator (2026-11-23) -- the fixed port with an environment override, the single instance found through one per-user record, newer-replaces-older over a token-protected shutdown with no event lost, the home view of every project, each session view confined to its project's reported root, and the brain override scoped to the project it was run from.
- User-settled observer design, relayed by the orchestrator (2026-11-22) -- no silent port move, replay-then-live with dedupe on session and order, Host check, token on every request, file confinement, delete of past sessions only, and no file watching.
- Observer feature parent spec and its event-log child spec (frontmatter) -- the recording side as the logs' only writer, file-first recording, and the rule that a live session cannot be deleted.
